Report ...) form specified at the end.\\\\\\\\nA fact carries the verbatim figures, benchmarks or architectural predicates of its source, and the evidence against a claim (failure modes, boundaries, negative tradeoffs, disconfirming sources) counts as much as the evidence for it; a report that only agrees is one-sided.\\\\\\\\nThe reply is read by an ASN parser: parentheses only; markdown code fences, JSON, brackets and backticks make it unreadable.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTopic: rust memory\\\\\\\\nDepth: flash\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSchema:\\\\\\\\n1. A fact is one atomic claim the source text states, with verbatim evidence, a metric or a definition from the distilled sources; a page title is not a fact.\\\\\\\\n2. Every fact has a predicate (:relation \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"verb\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" :subject \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"noun\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" :object \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"noun\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") whose subject is a specific thing the source states something about, never the whole topic.\\\\\\\\n3. Every fact has :source, the url of the Raw Evidence item it came from.\\\\\\\\n4. Epistemic classification:\\\\\\\\n   - verificationStatus: one of [:corroborated, :unfalsified, :disputed, :refuted].\\\\\\\\n     :empiricallyVerified is reserved for runner-executed test assertions; web text never carries it.\\\\\\\\n   - sourceKind: one of [:primaryArtifact, :peerReviewed, :crowdsourced, :secondaryReport, :unverifiedWeb].\\\\\\\\n5. Polarity:\\\\\\\\n   - isFalsifying: boolean (true or false).\\\\\\\\n   - At depth 'bipolar' or 'matrix' the facts hold at least one affirmative fact (:isFalsifying false) and at least one adversarial fact or limitation (:isFalsifying true).\\\\\\\\n6. A fact from a crawled web source ('crawled': true in Raw Evidence) carries :crawled true in its (:fact ...) form.\\\\\\\\n7. positiveSynthesis: 1-2 sentences summarizing what the facts support.\\\\\\\\n8. falsificationCritique: 1-2 sentences summarizing limitations, failure modes, counter-evidence, or edge cases the facts s